I've written a function that might be worth including in this package (or something similar). I sometimes end up in a situation where it's very difficult to distinguish between parts of a function inside the flame graph (especially because the bulk of my code is just loops, assignments, and basic operators), so I have to add code to help visually separate sections of the function so I can figure out which part actually needs optimization. To help with that, I've created the following function that I can call at key points in the code I'm trying to profile:

```
  delay <- function(text, t = 1) {

    assign(text, function() {
      t0 <- Sys.time()
      while (Sys.time() - t0 < t) { }
    })

    do.call(text, list())
  }
```

The `text` parameter gets assigned as a function name that will then show up in the flame graph. Here's an example:
![Screenshot from 2023-03-03 13-59-15](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/13956617/222804535-31addb8f-78f8-4187-b3be-f61e4d054ffd.png)

Basically, I called `delay("CRW Lookup")` and `delay("Build TR")` in places that delimit important sections. I thought my "CRW Lookup" section might be a possible bottleneck because it's a complex nested loop, but it turns out it's the opposite case, and there was no way I could ever figure this out based on the rest of the flame graph data.

Edit: calling it something like `marker()` might make more sense

Hi,

I stumbled upon a case in which R's lazy evaluation made me think my code was spending a lot of time in a function, when if fact, a totally different function was using my CPU cycles. I found out that this was due to lazy evaluation, as described in the [FAQ](https://rstudio.github.io/profvis/faq.html#why-is-the-call-stack-reversed-for-some-of-my-code).

Here is an example I used:
```R
my_fun <- function() {
  sum(ifelse(runif(10000) > 0.5, 0.8, 0.2) /
      ifelse(runif(10000) > 0.5, 0.1, 0.9))
}

example_1 <- function() {
  for (i in seq_len(100)) {
    print.default(my_fun())
  }
}

example_2 <- function() {
  for (i in seq_len(100)) {
    val <- my_fun()
    print.default(val)
  }
}

library(profvis)
print(profvis(example_1()))  # all time spent used by print (unexpected!)
print(profvis(example_2()))  # all time spent in my_fun (as expected)
```

When profiling, **R's lazy evaluation leads to results that are inaccurate and counter-productive**; how many people will lose time trying to optimize a function that runs fine? The profvis FAQ suggests to use intermediate variables as a workaround, but that assumes that i) the person is aware of the potential issue, and ii) is willing to go through the (perhaps large) codebase and fix all problematic occurrences. Instead, I think profivis should address the issue one way or another:
1. The easiest solution would be to turn off R's lazy evaluation, but it seems there is no way to do that. 
2. Another solution would be to fix the profiling results based on the call tree. In `example_1()` above, which uses lazy evaluation, I have verified using `getParseData(parse("my_example.R"))` that the parse tree is correct, i.e. that `print.default()` and `my_func()` are siblings called by the same parent function, `example_1()`. Thus, the parse tree seems well suited to rectify the profiling results.

Cheers,
Florent

