It looks like functions that are called via `x$foo()` don't get a label and show up as `<Anonymous>` in the profiler output. It would be nice if the profiler could be modified to record `"foo"` in such cases, though I don't know for sure whether it's possible. This would improve the output when ggproto, R6, and ref classes are used.

Presently, with this file `anonymous.R`:

``` R
foo <- function(x) {
  seq_len(x)
}

e <- environment()

for (i in 1:1e5) {
  foo(1000)
  e$foo(1000)
}
```

Called this way:

``` R
pa <- prof({
  source('anonymous.R')
}, prof_file = 'pa.prof')

profvis(pa)
```

The `foo` calls are labled as `"foo"`, but the `e$foo` calls are labeled as `"<Anonymous>"`.

Instead I think it should just be truncated - I think you want

``` css
td.code {
  white-space: pre-wrap; 
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
```

but I couldn't figure out quite the right selector to get it to work.

See https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/text-overflow and http://caniuse.com/#search=text-overflow
